{
  "image_name": "2cbbb97bddb6dc5d509a8aa2f5b61dcc.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how an activated myofibroblast drives collagen turnover and how this is reflected by blood biomarkers. It shows increased production of both type III and type I procollagen. On the type III side, procollagen is processed to type III collagen while releasing the propeptide PIIINP, which then enters the bloodstream and is shown as increased.\n\nOn the type I side, type I procollagen is cleaved by a procollagen carboxy-terminal proteinase (PCP) to form type I collagen molecules, which assemble into highly crosslinked type I collagen fibers. This processing also releases PICP, which appears in blood as increased. The diagram then highlights breakdown of these mature type I fibers by MMP1, generating the fragment CITP. However, because the fibers are highly crosslinked, less CITP seems to be released, and the blood ratio of CITP to MMP1 is shown as decreased.\n\nOverall, the image conveys a shift toward active fibrosis: myofibroblasts produce more collagen, circulating formation markers such as PIIINP and PICP rise, and degradation-related output from dense crosslinked type I collagen falls, reflected by lower CITP and a lower CITP:MMP1 ratio.",
